.TH mount "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
mount \- mount a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/mount [\fIspecial directory \fB[\-fru]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mount -- mount file system
Usage:	/etc/mount [ special directory [-fru] ]
Options:
	-r	Mount is read only
	-f	Force read/write even if not cleanly unmounted
	-w	Remount an already-mounted file system read/write
	-u	No mount, just update '/etc/mnttab' and/or '/etc/mtab'
The file system residing on the block special file 'special' becomes
accessible through the pathname 'directory'.
With no arguments, lists the currently mounted file systems.
If /etc/mtab appears out of date, due to reboot, the fact is noted.
If /etc/mnttab is present, then the dates of mounting will be reported.
.HE
.B mount
grafts the file system held on block-special device
.I special
onto the directory tree at
.IR directory .
With no arguments,
.B mount
prints the file systems now mounted: it reads
.B /etc/mnttab
if that file exists, and otherwise
.BR /etc/mtab .
.PP
Before mounting,
.B mount
reads the super block of
.I special
to check that it holds a properly formed file system and whether
that file system was cleanly unmounted.
If it was not, and neither
.B \-f
nor
.B \-w
was given,
.B mount
mounts it read-only and suggests running
.B check
on it; with
.B \-f
it forces a read/write mount instead.
.B \-w
leaves the clean/dirty decision to the kernel's
.B mount()
call, which itself refuses to mount a dirty file system read/write
unless forced.
.PP
After mounting (or, with
.BR \-u ,
without actually mounting anything),
.B mount
records the mount in
.B /etc/mnttab
(if it can be opened) and in
.BR /etc/mtab ,
replacing any existing entry for the same
.I special
and adding the mount time to
.BR /etc/mnttab .
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Mount
.I special
read-only.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Force a read/write mount even though
.I special
was not cleanly unmounted.
.IP \fB\-w\fR
Remount an already-mounted file system for read/write access,
leaving the clean/dirty decision to the kernel.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Do not mount anything; only add or update the entry for
.I special
in
.B /etc/mnttab
and
.BR /etc/mtab .
This is used at boot time to record the root file system, which the
kernel has already mounted.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/mtab\fR	table of mounted file systems
\fB/etc/mnttab\fR	table of mounted file systems, with mount times
\fB/etc/boottime\fR	time of the last reboot, used to detect a stale /etc/mtab
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkfs ,
.BR umount ,
.B mount()
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mount
complains if
.I special
is not a block-special device, if
.I directory
is not a directory, or if
.I special
does not hold a properly formed file system.
It exits nonzero if the underlying
.B mount()
system call fails, for example because the file system or directory
is already busy.
.SH NOTES
Mounting requires read and write access to
.I special
itself, which in practice restricts
.B mount
to the superuser, since block special files are normally not
writable by other users.
The
.B \-u
option updates the mount tables even though no
.B mount()
system call is made, so it should be used only when the kernel has
already mounted
.I special
by some other means.
